By Owen Scutt

- Using Asteroseismology to get mass, radius, age, internal structure from oscillation frequencies
- Using more dimensions increase code runnings costs when modelling
- Created Pitchfork - makes grid of stellar models of solar-like oscillators
- Grid modelling better but need to focus on uncertainties
- Surface of star needs to be accounted for to reduce the difference in observed and modelled oscillation frequencies
- Neural networks more efficient than interpolation methods and allows for higher dimensions including varied mixing and 
rotation
- Goal to emulate oscillation frequencies of solar-like stars with machine learning and then use Bayesian inference
- Methods - create a stellar model grid which is the training data, develop Pitchfork by teaching it how stellar parameters 
lead to observable quantities, work through the Bayesian inference that the emulator is embedded in, treat surface effect 
within model so its uncertainty is accounted for, test using hare and hounds where unseen data from training phase goes in to
recover hounds
- Results - Pitchfork is a high precision emulator, recovered stellar properties of test data with low bias, well calculated
 uncertainties, reliable posterior distributions, scalable for future work
